  "result": "The 30-minute lesson on O3 is written, with a short quiz, a cheat sheet and updated notes. I haven't opened the lesson in a browser or run any length check, so its fit to 30 minutes is only from my section plan.\n\n**Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`. It covers:\n- what the context window is and what counts toward it;\n- why the program re-sends the whole message list at every step, so cost grows and recall degrades;\n- ways to keep context small;\n- notes saved between sessions, using the memory tool as the example;\n- retrieval for the manuals.\n\nIt uses your laser cutter and manuals examples, has a warm-up recall question, two figures and four practice checks, and ends with a worked sorting example. The win is that you can sort each kind of information in the assistant into the system prompt, saved notes or a database, or a search tool.\n\nThe sources are Anthropic's context engineering post and the Claude docs on context windows and the memory tool, listed in `RESOURCES.md`. The token numbers in the cost example are invented to show the shape.\n\n**Quiz:** `quizzes/0002.json` has five items, with its key in `keys/0002.json`. It is ready when you want it; I have not shown any answers.\n\n**Cheat sheet:** `cheatsheets/context-and-memory.html`. I also added a line about the new lesson and quiz to `NOTES.md`.\n\nTwo things to know:\n- The lesson's suggestion that certifications live in a database rather than model-written notes is my design suggestion, not from the sources. It is marked that way in the lesson.\n- Quiz 0001 is still ungraded. Say if you want it graded before you take quiz 0002.\n\nDo you want a shorter or longer version of this lesson?",
